<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Batak</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Batak people</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Batak are a group of related peoples of the highlands of North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, centred on Lake Toba. They include the Toba, Karo, Simalungun, Pakpak, Angkola and Mandailing, each with its own language and customs. Long trading forest products with the coastal Malay sultanates, many became Christian through German missionaries in the 19th century, while others, notably in the south, are Muslim.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/batak.jpg" alt="Batak" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christian Advs Sltg · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Batak_WRB.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Batak is a collective term for closely related Austronesian peoples of North Sumatra and parts of adjacent provinces, who speak the Batak languages. It always includes the Toba, Karo, Simalungun, Pakpak-Dairi, Angkola and Mandailing. Northern Sumatra seems to have been settled by agriculturalists considerably later than the south. Although the Batak are often thought of as isolated because they live inland, there is evidence that they traded with neighbouring kingdoms for a thousand years or more. The 13th-century work of Zhao Rugua may mention a Ba-ta dependency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>, and the Suma Oriental, of the 15th century, refers to a kingdom of Bata between Pasai and Aru.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Batak may have supplied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camphor">camphor</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benzoin-resin">benzoin</a> and other forest products, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a>, for trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from perhaps the 8th or 9th century, for the next thousand years. Men carried the goods on their backs to ports, and the port of Barus on the west coast became known for camphor. Batak warriors were often hired as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> by neighbouring Malay courts. Contact with Tamil and Javanese traders and settlers over many centuries, including at the Padang Lawas temple complex, may have brought in Mahayana Buddhist, Shaivist and Tantrist practices.</p><p>Much of what is known of the older religion comes from German and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> missionaries, who took increasing interest in Batak beliefs towards the end of the 19th century. In the colonial era the Dutch introduced cash crops such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oil-palm">oil palm</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> and turned parts of Batak land into plantations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Batak societies are patriarchal and organised in clans called marga. The Toba believe that all marga descend from one ancestor, Si Raja Batak, and a genealogy, the tarombo, is important: a Batak who does not know their lineage is considered strayed. The philosophy of Dalihan Na Tolu, the three-legged furnace, calls for respect to the wife's family, kindness to women, and care in living with close relatives.</p><p>The traditional occupations were farming, hunting and fishing. The Batak have their own script, the Surat Batak, used chiefly in ceremonies. For the Toba and Karo, the tendi or tondi, a life-soul, matters more than the gods; after death the begu, the death-soul, continues near the village. Ritual cannibalism was well documented before the colonial period. Batak writers, among them Merari Siregar and Mochtar Lubis, helped shape modern Bahasa Indonesia and its literature.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Batak” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
